    question: "What is the difference between `update()` and `merge()`?",
    slug: "update-vs-merge-hibernate",
    shortDescription: "Compare update() and merge().",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`update()` reattaches a detached entity to the session and forces an update. `merge()` copies state from a detached entity to a managed entity (or creates a new one), and returns the managed instance. - `session.update(entity)`: Reattaches the entity to the current session. If the entity has been modified, it will be updated at flush. Throws exception if another entity with same ID is already in session. - `session.merge(entity)`: Copies the state of the detached entity to a new or existing managed instance. Returns the managed instance. Does not modify the detached object. Safer to avoid conflicts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `update()` and `merge()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `update()` reattaches a detached entity to the session and forces an update.\n2. `merge()` copies state from a detached entity to a managed entity (or creates a new one), and returns the managed instance.\n3. - `session.update(entity)`: Reattaches the entity to the current session.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `update()` reattaches a detached entity to the session and forces an update.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `update()` reattaches a detached entity to the session and forces an update.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
