    question: "What is lazy loading in Hibernate and how does it work?",
    slug: "lazy-loading-hibernate",
    shortDescription: "Explain lazy loading.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLazy loading is a strategy where associated entities are not loaded from the database until they are accessed. It is implemented using proxies or collections. It improves performance by reducing initial data loading but can cause `LazyInitializationException` if accessed outside the session. - Lazy loading is configured using `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` on relationships (default for `@OneToMany`, `@ManyToMany`). - Hibernate uses proxies for single-ended relationships and persistent collections for multi-valued. - When you access a lazy property, Hibernate initializes it with a DB query (if session is open). - If you access it after the session is closed, you get.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain lazy loading in Hibernate and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Lazy loading is a strategy where associated entities are not loaded from the database until they are accessed.\n2. It is implemented using proxies or collections.\n3. It improves performance by reducing initial data loading but can cause `LazyInitializationException` if accessed outside the session.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Lazy loading is a strategy where associated entities are not loaded from the database until they are accessed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Lazy loading is a strategy where associated entities are not loaded from the database until they are accessed.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
