    question: "What is the `N+1` problem in Hibernate and how do you solve it?",
    slug: "n-plus-one-problem",
    shortDescription: "Explain N+1 problem.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe N+1 problem occurs when you fetch a list of entities, and then for each entity, you fetch its lazy associations, resulting in N extra queries. It can be solved by using `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, `@EntityGraph`, or setting fetch mode to subselect. - If you have `List<Parent> parents = session.createQuery(\"from Parent\").list();` and then iterate and access `parent.getChildren()`, Hibernate fires a query for each parent's children (N queries). - Solutions: - `JOIN FETCH`: `from Parent p join fetch p.children` – loads children in the same query. - `@EntityGraph`: Define a graph to specify which associations to fetch. - `@BatchSize`: Use `@BatchSize(size=10)` on.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `N+1` problem in Hibernate and how do you solve it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The N+1 problem occurs when you fetch a list of entities, and then for each entity, you fetch its lazy associations, resulting in N extra queries.\n2. It can be solved by using `JOIN FETCH` in JPQL, `@EntityGraph`, or setting fetch mode to subselect.\n3. - If you have `List<Parent> parents = session.createQuery(\"from Parent\").list();` and then iterate and access `parent.getChildren()`, Hibernate fires a query for each parent's children (N queries).\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The N+1 problem occurs when you fetch a list of entities, and then for each entity, you fetch its lazy associations, resulting in N extra queries.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The N+1 problem occurs when you fetch a list of entities, and then for each entity, you fetch its lazy associations, resulting in N extra queries.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
