    question: "What is the `@Version` annotation in JPA?",
    slug: "version-annotation-jpa",
    shortDescription: "Explain @Version.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Version` is used for optimistic locking. It marks a field (usually numeric or timestamp) that is incremented each time an entity is updated. When updating, JPA checks if the version in the DB matches the version in the entity; if not, an `OptimisticLockException` is thrown. - Optimistic locking allows concurrent updates without locking, but detects conflicts at commit. - `@Version` on a numeric field (int, long) or timestamp. The value is automatically incremented or updated. - When an entity is updated, the version in the entity must match the database version; otherwise, an exception is thrown.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `@Version` annotation in JPA accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Version` is used for optimistic locking.\n2. It marks a field (usually numeric or timestamp) that is incremented each time an entity is updated.\n3. When updating, JPA checks if the version in the DB matches the version in the entity; if not, an `OptimisticLockException` is thrown.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `@Version` is used for optimistic locking.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@Version` is used for optimistic locking.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
