    question: "What are the different inheritance mapping strategies in JPA?",
    slug: "jpa-inheritance-strategies",
    shortDescription: "Explain JPA inheritance strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJPA supports three inheritance strategies: `SINGLE_TABLE` (all classes in one table with discriminator), `TABLE_PER_CLASS` (each class has its own table, no polymorphism in queries), and `JOINED` (subclass tables join with superclass table). - **SINGLE_TABLE**: All entities in hierarchy are stored in one table. Uses a discriminator column to differentiate types. Good performance (no joins), but columns for subclass attributes must be nullable. Usually the default. - **TABLE_PER_CLASS**: Each concrete class has its own table with all attributes (including inherited). No polymorphic queries across hierarchy (union needed). Less common. - **JOINED**: Superclass.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JPA supports three inheritance strategies: `SINGLE_TABLE` (all classes in one table with discriminator), `TABLE_PER_CLASS` (each class has its own table, no polymorphism in queries), and `JOINED` (subclass tables join with superclass table).\n2. - **SINGLE_TABLE**: All entities in hierarchy are stored in one table.\n3. Uses a discriminator column to differentiate types.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. JPA supports three inheritance strategies: `SINGLE_TABLE` (all classes in one table with discriminator), `TABLE_PER_CLASS` (each class has its own table, no polymorphism in queries), and `JOINED` (subclass tables join with superclass table).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Advanced Java, what are the different inheritance mapping strategies in jpa can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
