    slug: "responsebody-vs-responseentity",
    shortDescription: "Compare @ResponseBody and @ResponseEntity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@ResponseBody` indicates that the return value of a method should be written directly to the HTTP response body. `@ResponseEntity` represents the entire HTTP response, including status, headers, and body, offering more control. - `@ResponseBody`: Used on a method to serialize the return object to the response body (often JSON). - `@ResponseEntity<T>`: A class that allows you to set status code, headers, and body explicitly. Commonly used to return different status codes (e.g., 201 Created, 404 Not Found).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@responsebody` and `@responseentity` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@ResponseBody` indicates that the return value of a method should be written directly to the HTTP response body.\n2. `@ResponseEntity` represents the entire HTTP response, including status, headers, and body, offering more control.\n3. - `@ResponseBody`: Used on a method to serialize the return object to the response body (often JSON).\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@ResponseBody` indicates that the return value of a method should be written directly to the HTTP response body.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@ResponseBody` indicates that the return value of a method should be written directly to the HTTP response body.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
