    slug: "resttemplate",
    shortDescription: "Explain RestTemplate.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`RestTemplate` is a synchronous client for making HTTP requests in Spring. It provides methods like `getForObject()`, `postForObject()`, `exchange()` to interact with REST APIs. It's being replaced by `WebClient` for reactive. - `RestTemplate` is thread-safe after configuration. - You can set message converters, error handlers, interceptors. - It simplifies common operations like GET, POST, PUT, DELETE. - For asynchronous or reactive, use `WebClient`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `RestTemplate` and how is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `RestTemplate` is a synchronous client for making HTTP requests in Spring.\n2. It provides methods like `getForObject()`, `postForObject()`, `exchange()` to interact with REST APIs.\n3. It's being replaced by `WebClient` for reactive.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nA legacy Spring service using `RestTemplate` can be migrated incrementally to `RestClient` for synchronous calls, while reactive flows can use `WebClient`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `RestTemplate` is a synchronous client for making HTTP requests in Spring.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
