    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nService discovery is a pattern where services register themselves with a registry, and clients discover them. Eureka is a Netflix service registry used in Spring Cloud. Services register with Eureka server, and clients use Eureka client to locate services. - **Eureka Server**: registry that holds service instances. Each microservice registers with it. - **Eureka Client**: embedded in each service to register, renew lease, and query for other services. - Services periodically send heartbeats; if not, they are removed. - Clients use load balancing (Ribbon) to choose instance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain service discovery and how does Eureka accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Service discovery is a pattern where services register themselves with a registry, and clients discover them.\n2. Eureka is a Netflix service registry used in Spring Cloud.\n3. Services register with Eureka server, and clients use Eureka client to locate services.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Service discovery is a pattern where services register themselves with a registry, and clients discover them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Service discovery is a pattern where services register themselves with a registry, and clients discover them.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
