    question: "What is the difference between `@LoadBalanced` and `@EnableDiscoveryClient`?",
    slug: "loadbalanced-vs-discovery",
    shortDescription: "Compare annotations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@LoadBalanced` is used on a `RestTemplate` bean to enable client-side load balancing. `@EnableDiscoveryClient` enables service discovery. They often work together: the `RestTemplate` will use the service discovery client to resolve service names to actual instances and load balance. - `@LoadBalanced`: Marks a `RestTemplate` to use a `LoadBalancerInterceptor` that replaces service names with actual host:port using a load balancer (e.g., Ribbon). - `@EnableDiscoveryClient`: Enables discovery client, allowing the application to interact with the registry.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `@loadbalanced` and `@enablediscoveryclient` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@LoadBalanced` is used on a `RestTemplate` bean to enable client-side load balancing.\n2. `@EnableDiscoveryClient` enables service discovery.\n3. They often work together: the `RestTemplate` will use the service discovery client to resolve service names to actual instances and load balance.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `@LoadBalanced` is used on a `RestTemplate` bean to enable client-side load balancing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@LoadBalanced` is used on a `RestTemplate` bean to enable client-side load balancing.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
