    question: "What is the difference between a process and a thread?",
    slug: "process-vs-thread",
    shortDescription: "Compare process and thread.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process; threads share the process's memory and resources. - **Process**: Has own address space, file descriptors, and system resources. Inter-process communication is expensive. - **Thread**: Shares heap, code, and data with other threads in the same process. Communication is cheaper but requires synchronization.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a process and a thread and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space.\n2. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process; threads share the process's memory and resources.\n3. - **Process**: Has own address space, file descriptors, and system resources.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A process is an independent program in execution with its own memory space.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
