    slug: "create-thread-java",
    shortDescription: "List ways to create threads.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTwo ways: extending `Thread` class and overriding `run()`, or implementing `Runnable` interface and passing to `Thread`. The `Runnable` approach is preferred because it allows extending another class. - **Extend Thread**: `class MyThread extends Thread { public void run() {.} }` then `new MyThread().start()`. - **Implement Runnable**: `class MyRunnable implements Runnable { public void run() {.} }` then `new Thread(new MyRunnable()).start()`. - Also: using `Callable` and `FutureTask` for returning results. - With `ExecutorService` for thread pools.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Two ways: extending `Thread` class and overriding `run()`, or implementing `Runnable` interface and passing to `Thread`.\n2. The `Runnable` approach is preferred because it allows extending another class.\n3. - **Extend Thread**: `class MyThread extends Thread { public void run() {.} }` then `new MyThread().start()`.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Two ways: extending `Thread` class and overriding `run()`, or implementing `Runnable` interface and passing to `Thread`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Advanced Java, what are the ways to create a thread in java can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
