    question: "What is the difference between `wait()`, `notify()`, and `notifyAll()`?",
    slug: "wait-notify",
    shortDescription: "Explain wait/notify.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`wait()` causes the current thread to wait until another thread calls `notify()` or `notifyAll()` on the same object. `notify()` wakes up one waiting thread, `notifyAll()` wakes all. They must be called from a synchronized context. - `wait()`: releases the lock and waits. It can be called with timeout. - `notify()`: wakes a single thread waiting on the same object's monitor (arbitrary). - `notifyAll()`: wakes all waiting threads. - They are methods of `Object`. Must be called from `synchronized` block/method.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `wait()`, `notify()`, and `notifyall()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `wait()` causes the current thread to wait until another thread calls `notify()` or `notifyAll()` on the same object.\n2. `notify()` wakes up one waiting thread, `notifyAll()` wakes all.\n3. They must be called from a synchronized context.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `wait()` causes the current thread to wait until another thread calls `notify()` or `notifyAll()` on the same object.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `wait()` causes the current thread to wait until another thread calls `notify()` or `notifyAll()` on the same object.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
