    slug: "lock-vs-synchronized",
    shortDescription: "Compare Lock and synchronized.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`Lock` (from `java.util.concurrent.locks`) offers more flexible locking than `synchronized`. It provides `tryLock()`, `lockInterruptibly()`, and can be unlocked in a different scope. It also supports fairness policies. - `synchronized`: implicit locking, easy to use, but not flexible; cannot interrupt, no try-lock, no timeout. - `Lock`: explicit locking with `lock()` and `unlock()` (must be in finally block). Provides `tryLock()` (non-blocking), `tryLock(long, TimeUnit)`, and `lockInterruptibly()`. Can be fair.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `lock` and `synchronized` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `Lock` (from `java.util.concurrent.locks`) offers more flexible locking than `synchronized`.\n2. It provides `tryLock()`, `lockInterruptibly()`, and can be unlocked in a different scope.\n3. It also supports fairness policies.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `Lock` (from `java.util.concurrent.locks`) offers more flexible locking than `synchronized`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `Lock` (from `java.util.concurrent.locks`) offers more flexible locking than `synchronized`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
