    question: "What is the difference between `ConcurrentHashMap` and `Hashtable`?",
    slug: "concurrenthashmap-vs-hashtable",
    shortDescription: "Compare ConcurrentHashMap and Hashtable.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`ConcurrentHashMap` is a thread-safe map that provides higher concurrency by using lock striping (segmented locks). `Hashtable` is synchronized (single lock) and is legacy. `ConcurrentHashMap` allows concurrent reads and writes without full locking. - `Hashtable`: synchronized methods, only one thread can access at a time, poor performance. - `ConcurrentHashMap`: uses bucket-level locks (or CAS in Java 8+), supports high concurrency. It does not allow `null` keys or values. - `ConcurrentHashMap` is preferred in concurrent environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `concurrenthashmap` and `hashtable` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `ConcurrentHashMap` is a thread-safe map that provides higher concurrency by using lock striping (segmented locks).\n2. `Hashtable` is synchronized (single lock) and is legacy.\n3. `ConcurrentHashMap` allows concurrent reads and writes without full locking.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nA `HashMap<Long, Employee>` is suitable for expected O(1) lookup when a map is not concurrently mutated; use synchronization or a concurrent collection when shared writes are required.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `ConcurrentHashMap` is a thread-safe map that provides higher concurrency by using lock striping (segmented locks).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
