    question: "What is the JVM and what is its architecture?",
    slug: "jvm-architecture",
    shortDescription: "Explain JVM architecture.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the runtime environment for Java bytecode. Its architecture includes: Class Loader, Runtime Data Areas (Method Area, Heap, Stack, PC Registers, Native Method Stack), Execution Engine (Interpreter, JIT Compiler), and Garbage Collector. - **Class Loader**: loads.class files. - **Runtime Data Areas**: - Method Area: class metadata, static variables. - Heap: all objects and arrays. - Stack: per-thread, stores frames (local variables, operand stack). - PC Registers: program counter per thread. - Native Method Stack: for native methods. - **Execution Engine**: interprets bytecode; JIT compiles hot code to native for.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the JVM and what is its architecture accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the runtime environment for Java bytecode.\n2. Its architecture includes: Class Loader, Runtime Data Areas (Method Area, Heap, Stack, PC Registers, Native Method Stack), Execution Engine (Interpreter, JIT Compiler), and Garbage Collector.\n3. - **Class Loader**: loads.class files.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the runtime environment for Java bytecode.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is the runtime environment for Java bytecode.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
