    question: "What is the difference between heap and stack in Java?",
    slug: "heap-vs-stack",
    shortDescription: "Compare heap and stack.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHeap is the runtime data area where objects and arrays are allocated; it is shared by all threads. Stack is per-thread and stores method invocation frames (local variables, operands). Heap is managed by GC; stack is automatically freed. - **Heap**: larger, slower, garbage collected, shared, stores objects. - **Stack**: smaller, faster, LIFO, per-thread, stores primitive values and references to objects. - Stack overflow when too many nested calls; heap overflow when memory exhausted.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between heap and stack in java and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Heap is the runtime data area where objects and arrays are allocated; it is shared by all threads.\n2. Stack is per-thread and stores method invocation frames (local variables, operands).\n3. Heap is managed by GC; stack is automatically freed.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Heap is the runtime data area where objects and arrays are allocated; it is shared by all threads.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Heap is the runtime data area where objects and arrays are allocated; it is shared by all threads.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
