    slug: "garbage-collectors",
    shortDescription: "List Java garbage collectors.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJava has several garbage collectors: Serial, Parallel (Throughput), CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep, deprecated), G1, and the latest ZGC and Shenandoah. Each has different trade-offs in throughput, latency, and footprint. - **Serial**: single-threaded, for small applications. - **Parallel**: multi-threaded, focuses on throughput. - **CMS**: low pause, concurrent, deprecated in Java 9. - **G1**: default since Java 9, balanced, region-based, good for larger heaps. - **ZGC**: low latency, scalable to large heaps. - **Shenandoah**: low pause, concurrent.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Java has several garbage collectors: Serial, Parallel (Throughput), CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep, deprecated), G1, and the latest ZGC and Shenandoah.\n2. Each has different trade-offs in throughput, latency, and footprint.\n3. - **Serial**: single-threaded, for small applications.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Java has several garbage collectors: Serial, Parallel (Throughput), CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep, deprecated), G1, and the latest ZGC and Shenandoah.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an application built with Advanced Java, what are the different types of garbage collectors in java can affect runtime behavior or maintainability. Check the language/runtime or framework version before relying on a version-sensitive detail.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
