    question: "What is the `finalize()` method and why is it deprecated?",
    slug: "finalize-method",
    shortDescription: "Explain finalize().",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`finalize()` was a method called by GC before reclaiming an object, used for cleanup. It's deprecated because it's unpredictable, can cause performance issues, and can be misused. Use `try-with-resources` or `Cleaner` instead. - `finalize()` is called at most once by GC. - It can resurrect objects; it's slow and not guaranteed to run. - Alternatives: `try-with-resources` for auto-closeable resources, `Cleaner` for native resources.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `finalize()` method and why is it deprecated accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `finalize()` was a method called by GC before reclaiming an object, used for cleanup.\n2. It's deprecated because it's unpredictable, can cause performance issues, and can be misused.\n3. Use `try-with-resources` or `Cleaner` instead.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `finalize()` was a method called by GC before reclaiming an object, used for cleanup.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `finalize()` was a method called by GC before reclaiming an object, used for cleanup.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
