    question: "What is a memory leak in Java and how can you detect it?",
    slug: "memory-leak-java",
    shortDescription: "Explain memory leak.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA memory leak occurs when objects are no longer used but still referenced, preventing GC from reclaiming them. Causes: static collections, unclosed resources, listeners not removed, etc. Detection: using profilers (VisualVM, JProfiler) and heap dumps. - Common causes: listeners added but never removed; caches with no eviction; static maps holding objects; thread locals not cleared. - Detection: use profiling tools to monitor heap, take heap dumps, analyze with MAT or JVisualVM. - Prevention: use weak references, ensure cleanup in finally blocks, use `try-with-resources`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a memory leak in Java and how can you detect it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A memory leak occurs when objects are no longer used but still referenced, preventing GC from reclaiming them.\n2. Causes: static collections, unclosed resources, listeners not removed, etc.\n3. Detection: using profilers (VisualVM, JProfiler) and heap dumps.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A memory leak occurs when objects are no longer used but still referenced, preventing GC from reclaiming them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A memory leak occurs when objects are no longer used but still referenced, preventing GC from reclaiming them.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
