    slug: "factory-vs-abstractfactory",
    shortDescription: "Compare Factory and Abstract Factory.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFactory Method creates a single product via inheritance. Abstract Factory creates families of related products via composition. Abstract Factory is a factory of factories. - **Factory Method**: relies on subclasses to decide which concrete product to create. One method to create one product. - **Abstract Factory**: provides an interface for creating multiple types of related products. Often implemented using Factory Method itself.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `factory` and `abstract factory` patterns and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Factory Method creates a single product via inheritance.\n2. Abstract Factory creates families of related products via composition.\n3. Abstract Factory is a factory of factories.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Factory Method creates a single product via inheritance.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Factory Method creates a single product via inheritance.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
