    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA functional interface is an interface with exactly one abstract method. It can have default and static methods. It's used as the target type for lambda expressions. Examples: `Runnable`, `Callable`, `Predicate`, `Function`. - Annotated with `@FunctionalInterface` (optional). - The compiler checks that it has only one abstract method. - Many in `java.util.function` package.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a functional interface accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A functional interface is an interface with exactly one abstract method.\n2. It can have default and static methods.\n3. It's used as the target type for lambda expressions.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can depend on an interface such as `PaymentGateway`, allowing a production adapter and a test double to implement the same contract.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A functional interface is an interface with exactly one abstract method.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
