    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@SpringBootTest` is an annotation for integration testing in Spring Boot. It loads the full application context and allows testing the application as a whole. It can be used with `@Test` and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` for web layer testing. - It loads the entire context. - Can be configured with `webEnvironment` (MOCK, RANDOM_PORT, DEFINED_PORT). - Used with `@MockBean` to replace beans with mocks. - Slower than unit tests, but tests integration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `@SpringBootTest` annotation accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@SpringBootTest` is an annotation for integration testing in Spring Boot.\n2. It loads the full application context and allows testing the application as a whole.\n3. It can be used with `@Test` and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` for web layer testing.\n4. Distinguish language/runtime guarantees from framework conventions and call out version-sensitive behavior when it changes the answer..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an enterprise Java application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `@SpringBootTest` is an annotation for integration testing in Spring Boot.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an enterprise Java application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `@SpringBootTest` is an annotation for integration testing in Spring Boot.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Mixing Java language guarantees with framework conventions.\n- Giving a version-sensitive API recommendation without a version note.\n- Ignoring concurrency, resource management, or error handling when it affects the topic.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the version assumption when the API or runtime behavior is version-sensitive. Then explain the trade-off that matters to the design.",
