    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe lifecycle includes: onCreate, onStart, onResume, onPause, onStop, onDestroy. Methods are called as the activity transitions between states. Developers override these to manage resources and state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the activity lifecycle accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The lifecycle includes: onCreate, onStart, onResume, onPause, onStop, onDestroy.\n2. Methods are called as the activity transitions between states.\n3. Developers override these to manage resources and state.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA screen can move through `onCreate`, `onStart`, `onResume`, `onPause`, and `onStop`; initialization and cleanup should follow the lifecycle of the resource being managed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. The lifecycle includes: onCreate, onStart, onResume, onPause, onStop, onDestroy.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
