    shortDescription: "Compare intent types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nExplicit intents target a specific component (class name). Implicit intents declare an action and data type, and the system finds an appropriate component (e.g., open a web page).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between explicit and implicit intents and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Explicit intents target a specific component (class name).\n2. Implicit intents declare an action and data type, and the system finds an appropriate component (e.g., open a web page).\n3. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Explicit intents target a specific component (class name).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Explicit intents target a specific component (class name).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
