    shortDescription: "Compare service types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Service is an Android component and, unless you arrange otherwise, its lifecycle callbacks and work execute on the app's main thread, so long-running work must not block that thread. IntentService was a convenience subclass that processed start intents sequentially on a worker thread and stopped itself after the queue was empty. IntentService was deprecated in API 30. For modern deferrable or guaranteed background work, prefer WorkManager; for an ongoing user-visible task that must continue while the user is aware of it, use an appropriately declared foreground service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a service and an intent service and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Service is an Android component and, unless you arrange otherwise, its lifecycle callbacks and work execute on the app's main thread, so long-running work must not block that thread.\n2. IntentService was a convenience subclass that processed start intents sequentially on a worker thread and stopped itself after the queue was empty.\n3. IntentService was deprecated in API 30.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. A Service is an Android component and, unless you arrange otherwise, its lifecycle callbacks and work execute on the app's main thread, so long-running work must not block that thread.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A Service is an Android component and, unless you arrange otherwise, its lifecycle callbacks and work execute on the app's main thread, so long-running work must not block that thread.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
