    shortDescription: "Describe each callback.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nonCreate: initial creation, set layout, bind data. onStart: activity becomes visible. onResume: activity is in foreground and interactive. onPause: activity loses focus but still visible (e.g., dialog). onStop: activity hidden. onDestroy: activity destroyed (finish or system reclaim).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the activity lifecycle in detail. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. onCreate: initial creation, set layout, bind data.\n2. onStart: activity becomes visible.\n3. onResume: activity is in foreground and interactive.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA screen can move through `onCreate`, `onStart`, `onResume`, `onPause`, and `onStop`; initialization and cleanup should follow the lifecycle of the resource being managed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, or maintainability. onCreate: initial creation, set layout, bind data.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
