    slug: "activity-rotation",
    shortDescription: "Explain configuration change.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration. Use ViewModel for data that should survive configuration changes, and use the saved-state mechanism such as onSaveInstanceState for small, transient UI state that must be restored. Do not use android:configChanges simply to avoid recreation; it makes the Activity responsible for handling configuration changes correctly and is appropriate only for specific, justified cases.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What happens when an activity is rotated accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration.\n2. Use ViewModel for data that should survive configuration changes, and use the saved-state mechanism such as onSaveInstanceState for small, transient UI state that must be restored.\n3. Do not use android:configChanges simply to avoid recreation; it makes the Activity responsible for handling configuration changes correctly and is appropriate only for specific, justified cases.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. A configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android app, what happens when an activity is rotated matters because lifecycle changes, process death, user navigation, and platform background limits can change the behavior that actually reaches the user.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration. Use ViewModel for data that should survive configuration changes, and use the saved-state mechanism such as onSaveInstanceState for small, transient UI state that must be restored. Do not use android:configChanges simply to avoid recreation; it makes the Activity responsible for handling configuration changes correctly and is appropriate only for specific, justified cases.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain What happens when an activity is rotated accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration.\n2. Use ViewModel for data that should survive configuration changes, and use the saved-state mechanism such as onSaveInstanceState for small, transient UI state that must be restored.\n3. Do not use android:configChanges simply to avoid recreation; it makes the Activity responsible for handling configuration changes correctly and is appropriate only for specific, justified cases.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. A configuration change such as rotation normally causes the current Activity instance to be destroyed and a new instance to be created with the new configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android app, what happens when an activity is rotated matters because lifecycle changes, process death, user navigation, and platform background limits can change the behavior that actually reaches the user.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
