    slug: "pass-data-between-activities",
    shortDescription: "Explain data passing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `Intent.putExtra()` to pass primitive data, serializable/parcelable objects. Use `startActivityForResult()` (deprecated) or `ActivityResultLauncher` for returning data. For large data, use ViewModel or shared repository.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you pass data between activities, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `Intent.putExtra()` to pass primitive data, serializable/parcelable objects.\n2. Use `startActivityForResult()` (deprecated) or `ActivityResultLauncher` for returning data.\n3. For large data, use ViewModel or shared repository.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Use `Intent.putExtra()` to pass primitive data, serializable/parcelable objects.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Use `Intent.putExtra()` to pass primitive data, serializable/parcelable objects.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
