    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLaunch modes define how an activity is launched: standard (default), singleTop (avoid new instance if on top), singleTask (single instance, clears above), singleInstance (single instance, no other activities).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Launch modes define how an activity is launched: standard (default), singleTop (avoid new instance if on top), singleTask (single instance, clears above), singleInstance (single instance, no other activities).\n2. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Launch modes define how an activity is launched: standard (default), singleTop (avoid new instance if on top), singleTask (single instance, clears above), singleInstance (single instance, no other activities).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android app, what are launch modes in android matters because lifecycle changes, process death, user navigation, and platform background limits can change the behavior that actually reaches the user.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
