    slug: "onsaveinstance-vs-onpause",
    shortDescription: "Compare state saving.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`onSaveInstanceState()` is called before the activity may be destroyed to save transient state (e.g., user input). It is used for configuration changes. `onPause()` is for persistent data (e.g., database writes).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `onsaveinstancestate()` and `onpause()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `onSaveInstanceState()` is called before the activity may be destroyed to save transient state (e.g., user input).\n2. It is used for configuration changes.\n3. `onPause()` is for persistent data (e.g., database writes).\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. `onSaveInstanceState()` is called before the activity may be destroyed to save transient state (e.g., user input).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `onSaveInstanceState()` is called before the activity may be destroyed to save transient state (e.g., user input).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
