    question: "How do you retain a fragment across configuration changes?",
    slug: "retain-fragment",
    shortDescription: "Explain retaining fragments.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDo not use setRetainInstance(true) in modern AndroidX Fragment code; it is deprecated. Keep retained state in a ViewModel scoped to the Fragment, Activity, or navigation graph. The Fragment itself should be recreated normally during configuration changes, while the ViewModel survives the Activity/Fragment recreation until its scope is finished. This separation also reduces lifecycle and memory-leak problems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you retain a fragment across configuration changes, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Do not use setRetainInstance(true) in modern AndroidX Fragment code; it is deprecated.\n2. Keep retained state in a ViewModel scoped to the Fragment, Activity, or navigation graph.\n3. The Fragment itself should be recreated normally during configuration changes, while the ViewModel survives the Activity/Fragment recreation until its scope is finished.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Do not use setRetainInstance(true) in modern AndroidX Fragment code; it is deprecated.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Do not use setRetainInstance(true) in modern AndroidX Fragment code; it is deprecated.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
