    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA FragmentTransaction groups fragment operations such as add, replace, remove, show, and hide. commit() schedules the transaction asynchronously on the main thread; commitNow() executes it immediately when allowed. addToBackStack() records the transaction so the user can navigate back through it. commitAllowingStateLoss() should be avoided unless losing that transaction is explicitly acceptable, because it can discard UI state if the transaction is committed after state has been saved.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `FragmentTransaction` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A FragmentTransaction groups fragment operations such as add, replace, remove, show, and hide.\n2. commit() schedules the transaction asynchronously on the main thread; commitNow() executes it immediately when allowed.\n3. addToBackStack() records the transaction so the user can navigate back through it.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. A FragmentTransaction groups fragment operations such as add, replace, remove, show, and hide.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. A FragmentTransaction groups fragment operations such as add, replace, remove, show, and hide.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
