    slug: "fragment-back-button",
    shortDescription: "Explain handling back.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFor Fragment-based navigation, prefer the Navigation component or FragmentManager's back stack rather than overriding the legacy onBackPressed callback. With FragmentManager, call addToBackStack() when you want a transaction to be reversible and popBackStack() to return to the previous state. Modern Android apps should also account for predictive back and use the supported back-navigation APIs instead of relying on deprecated onBackPressed interception.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle the back button for fragments, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. For Fragment-based navigation, prefer the Navigation component or FragmentManager's back stack rather than overriding the legacy onBackPressed callback.\n2. With FragmentManager, call addToBackStack() when you want a transaction to be reversible and popBackStack() to return to the previous state.\n3. Modern Android apps should also account for predictive back and use the supported back-navigation APIs instead of relying on deprecated onBackPressed interception.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. For Fragment-based navigation, prefer the Navigation component or FragmentManager's back stack rather than overriding the legacy onBackPressed callback.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. For Fragment-based navigation, prefer the Navigation component or FragmentManager's back stack rather than overriding the legacy onBackPressed callback.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
