    question: "What is a RecyclerView and how does it work?",
    slug: "recyclerview",
    shortDescription: "Explain RecyclerView.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRecyclerView displays large or changing collections by reusing a limited number of item views. A LayoutManager decides how items are positioned, an Adapter creates and binds ViewHolders, and the RecyclerView recycles item views as they leave the viewport. DiffUtil or ListAdapter can calculate minimal list changes instead of refreshing every row. For production apps, also consider stable item identity, efficient binding, paging for very large datasets, and avoiding expensive work in onBindViewHolder.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a RecyclerView and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. RecyclerView displays large or changing collections by reusing a limited number of item views.\n2. A LayoutManager decides how items are positioned, an Adapter creates and binds ViewHolders, and the RecyclerView recycles item views as they leave the viewport.\n3. DiffUtil or ListAdapter can calculate minimal list changes instead of refreshing every row.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. RecyclerView displays large or changing collections by reusing a limited number of item views.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. RecyclerView displays large or changing collections by reusing a limited number of item views.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
