    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCall `startService(Intent)` or `bindService()`. `startService` starts a service unconditionally; `bindService` binds to it for client-server interaction.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you start a service, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Call `startService(Intent)` or `bindService()`.\n2. `startService` starts a service unconditionally; `bindService` binds to it for client-server interaction.\n3. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Call `startService(Intent)` or `bindService()`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Call `startService(Intent)` or `bindService()`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
