    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWorkManager is a library for deferrable background work. It handles compatibility, battery constraints, and retries. It is recommended over `AsyncTask`, `IntentService`, and `JobScheduler`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `WorkManager` and why is it preferred accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. WorkManager is a library for deferrable background work.\n2. It handles compatibility, battery constraints, and retries.\n3. It is recommended over `AsyncTask`, `IntentService`, and `JobScheduler`.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nA sync task that should eventually run even if the app leaves the foreground can be scheduled with WorkManager, with constraints such as network availability applied to the work request.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. WorkManager is a library for deferrable background work.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
