    slug: "sharedpreferences-vs-preferences",
    shortDescription: "Compare methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\ngetSharedPreferences(name, mode) lets an application access a named SharedPreferences file from a Context, so an app can maintain multiple files. Activity.getPreferences(mode) is a convenience for one preference file associated with that Activity. Neither API should be described as generally deprecated. For new small persistent key-value storage, Android recommends considering Jetpack DataStore instead of SharedPreferences because DataStore provides asynchronous, transactional access through Kotlin coroutines and Flow.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `getsharedpreferences()` and `getpreferences()` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. getSharedPreferences(name, mode) lets an application access a named SharedPreferences file from a Context, so an app can maintain multiple files.\n2. Activity.getPreferences(mode) is a convenience for one preference file associated with that Activity.\n3. Neither API should be described as generally deprecated.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. getSharedPreferences(name, mode) lets an application access a named SharedPreferences file from a Context, so an app can maintain multiple files.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. getSharedPreferences(name, mode) lets an application access a named SharedPreferences file from a Context, so an app can maintain multiple files.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
