    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`@Query` is used to define custom SQL queries in DAO. It returns the result as a LiveData, Flow, or list. Example: `@Query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id =:id\")`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `@Query` annotation in Room accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `@Query` is used to define custom SQL queries in DAO.\n2. It returns the result as a LiveData, Flow, or list.\n3. Example: `@Query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id =:id\")`.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. `@Query` is used to define custom SQL queries in DAO.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. `@Query` is used to define custom SQL queries in DAO.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
