    shortDescription: "Compare storage types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInternal app-specific storage is private to the application and is a good place for sensitive or app-only files. External storage is not simply synonymous with a public SD card anymore: Android provides app-specific external directories and shared storage. For shared media, use MediaStore; for user-selected documents and other files, use the Storage Access Framework. Modern Android uses scoped storage, so broad filesystem access should not be treated as the normal solution.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between internal and external storage and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Internal app-specific storage is private to the application and is a good place for sensitive or app-only files.\n2. External storage is not simply synonymous with a public SD card anymore: Android provides app-specific external directories and shared storage.\n3. For shared media, use MediaStore; for user-selected documents and other files, use the Storage Access Framework.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Internal app-specific storage is private to the application and is a good place for sensitive or app-only files.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Internal app-specific storage is private to the application and is a good place for sensitive or app-only files.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
