    question: "What is the difference between `http://` and `https://`?",
    slug: "http-vs-https",
    shortDescription: "Compare protocols.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHTTP sends requests without transport encryption, while HTTPS uses TLS to provide confidentiality and server authentication and to protect data against tampering in transit. Android 9/API 28 and higher disable cleartext traffic by default for apps targeting API 28+, although an app can explicitly opt in through its network security configuration. Production applications should use HTTPS for sensitive and ordinary API traffic and should not disable certificate validation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `http://` and `https://` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. HTTP sends requests without transport encryption, while HTTPS uses TLS to provide confidentiality and server authentication and to protect data against tampering in transit.\n2. Android 9/API 28 and higher disable cleartext traffic by default for apps targeting API 28+, although an app can explicitly opt in through its network security configuration.\n3. Production applications should use HTTPS for sensitive and ordinary API traffic and should not disable certificate validation.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. HTTP sends requests without transport encryption, while HTTPS uses TLS to provide confidentiality and server authentication and to protect data against tampering in transit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. HTTP sends requests without transport encryption, while HTTPS uses TLS to provide confidentiality and server authentication and to protect data against tampering in transit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
