    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRetrofit does not universally mean that every suspend call runs on Dispatchers.IO. A suspend Retrofit API should be called from an appropriate coroutine context, and the application should choose the dispatcher according to the underlying work and architecture. Traditional Call.enqueue() is asynchronous and delivers callbacks according to the configured Retrofit callback executor; Android projects commonly configure this through the library's normal setup. The key interview point is to avoid blocking the main thread and to keep threading decisions explicit.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle threading in retrofit, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Retrofit does not universally mean that every suspend call runs on Dispatchers.IO.\n2. A suspend Retrofit API should be called from an appropriate coroutine context, and the application should choose the dispatcher according to the underlying work and architecture.\n3. Traditional Call.enqueue() is asynchronous and delivers callbacks according to the configured Retrofit callback executor; Android projects commonly configure this through the library's normal setup.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. Retrofit does not universally mean that every suspend call runs on Dispatchers.IO.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. Retrofit does not universally mean that every suspend call runs on Dispatchers.IO.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
