    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMVP (Model-View-Presenter): Presenter holds logic, updates View. MVVM: ViewModel exposes observable state; View observes. MVVM is better for lifecycle management.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between mvvm and mvp and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. MVP (Model-View-Presenter): Presenter holds logic, updates View.\n2. MVVM: ViewModel exposes observable state; View observes.\n3. MVVM is better for lifecycle management.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. MVP (Model-View-Presenter): Presenter holds logic, updates View.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. MVP (Model-View-Presenter): Presenter holds logic, updates View.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
