    slug: "viewmodel-class",
    shortDescription: "Explain ViewModel.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nViewModel is a class that stores UI-related data and survives configuration changes. It is scoped to the activity/fragment lifecycle. Use with `ViewModelProvider`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `ViewModel` class accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ViewModel is a class that stores UI-related data and survives configuration changes.\n2. It is scoped to the activity/fragment lifecycle.\n3. Use with `ViewModelProvider`.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA screen-scoped `ViewModel` can hold UI state across rotation while a repository performs data access; persistent storage is still needed for state that must survive process death.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. ViewModel is a class that stores UI-related data and survives configuration changes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
