    slug: "livedata",
    shortDescription: "Explain LiveData.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLiveData is an observable data holder that is lifecycle-aware. It updates observers only when the lifecycle is active (STARTED). It prevents memory leaks and crashes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `LiveData` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. LiveData is an observable data holder that is lifecycle-aware.\n2. It updates observers only when the lifecycle is active (STARTED).\n3. It prevents memory leaks and crashes.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. LiveData is an observable data holder that is lifecycle-aware.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. LiveData is an observable data holder that is lifecycle-aware.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Ignoring lifecycle changes or process death.\n- Assuming background work can run indefinitely.\n- Updating UI state outside the appropriate lifecycle-aware boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nBring the lifecycle or platform constraint into the answer when relevant. Android behavior often depends on what survives configuration changes and process death.",
