    question: "What is LiveData and its advantages?",
    slug: "livedata-advantages",
    shortDescription: "List LiveData benefits.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLiveData is a lifecycle-aware observable data holder. Observers associated with a LifecycleOwner receive updates only while their lifecycle is in an active state, which reduces common UI lifecycle mistakes. LiveData does not automatically eliminate every null or memory-leak problem, and it does not replace proper lifecycle management. It is particularly useful in existing View-based Android applications, while modern Kotlin-first applications often expose StateFlow or Flow from ViewModels.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what is livedata and its advantages. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. LiveData is a lifecycle-aware observable data holder.\n2. Observers associated with a LifecycleOwner receive updates only while their lifecycle is in an active state, which reduces common UI lifecycle mistakes.\n3. LiveData does not automatically eliminate every null or memory-leak problem, and it does not replace proper lifecycle management.\n4. Account for lifecycle, configuration changes, process death, and background-execution constraints when the topic touches application state or work..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with why an engineer would want the approach, then cover the operational or design cost that comes with it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an Android application, the behavior can be observed while the app starts, stops, recreates UI state, or processes background work. LiveData is a lifecycle-aware observable data holder.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an Android application, this affects runtime behavior, UI state, performance, accessibility, or maintainability depending on the feature. LiveData is a lifecycle-aware observable data holder.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
