    slug: "placement-group",
    shortDescription: "Explain clustering.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another. AWS supports Cluster for low-latency/high-throughput communication within an Availability Zone, Spread for placing a small number of critical instances on distinct hardware, Partition for isolating groups across separate racks, and Precision time for supported time-sensitive workloads.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an EC2 placement group accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another.\n2. AWS supports Cluster for low-latency/high-throughput communication within an Availability Zone, Spread for placing a small number of critical instances on distinct hardware, Partition for isolating groups across separate racks, and Precision time for supported time-sensitive workloads.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. An EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. An EC2 placement group controls how instances are physically positioned relative to one another.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing services that operate at different layers.\n- Omitting the request or data flow between services.\n- Ignoring IAM, networking, availability, or cost when those constraints affect the answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nSeparate the service’s responsibility from the surrounding architecture. Mention the key boundary or limitation instead of describing AWS as a collection of interchangeable services.",
