    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSpot Instances use spare EC2 capacity at a discount and can be interrupted when AWS needs the capacity. For many interruption types, EC2 provides a two-minute interruption notice. They are best for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads such as batch processing, distributed data processing, CI jobs, and stateless workers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a Spot Instance and when would you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Spot Instances use spare EC2 capacity at a discount and can be interrupted when AWS needs the capacity.\n2. For many interruption types, EC2 provides a two-minute interruption notice.\n3. They are best for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads such as batch processing, distributed data processing, CI jobs, and stateless workers.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Spot Instances use spare EC2 capacity at a discount and can be interrupted when AWS needs the capacity.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. Spot Instances use spare EC2 capacity at a discount and can be interrupted when AWS needs the capacity.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing services that operate at different layers.\n- Omitting the request or data flow between services.\n- Ignoring IAM, networking, availability, or cost when those constraints affect the answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nSeparate the service’s responsibility from the surrounding architecture. Mention the key boundary or limitation instead of describing AWS as a collection of interchangeable services.",
