    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nS3 is object storage (files) accessible via HTTP/HTTPS, multi-AZ, and ideal for static assets. EBS is block storage for EC2, attached to one instance, and used for databases and file systems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between s3 and ebs and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. S3 is object storage (files) accessible via HTTP/HTTPS, multi-AZ, and ideal for static assets.\n2. EBS is block storage for EC2, attached to one instance, and used for databases and file systems.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. S3 is object storage (files) accessible via HTTP/HTTPS, multi-AZ, and ideal for static assets.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. S3 is object storage (files) accessible via HTTP/HTTPS, multi-AZ, and ideal for static assets.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
