    slug: "public-vs-private-subnet",
    shortDescription: "Explain the distinction.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway (IGW), allowing instances to communicate with the internet. A private subnet does not have a direct route to the internet; it may use a NAT Gateway for outbound internet access.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a public subnet and a private subnet and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway (IGW), allowing instances to communicate with the internet.\n2. A private subnet does not have a direct route to the internet; it may use a NAT Gateway for outbound internet access.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. A public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway (IGW), allowing instances to communicate with the internet.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. A public subnet has a route to an Internet Gateway (IGW), allowing instances to communicate with the internet.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
