    question: "What is the difference between AWS Lambda and EC2?",
    slug: "lambda-vs-ec2",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEC2 is a virtual server; you manage the OS, patching, and scaling. Lambda is event-driven, automatically scales, and has a short execution timeout (15 min). Lambda is ideal for event-driven, short-lived tasks; EC2 for long-running, stateful apps.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between aws lambda and ec2 and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. EC2 is a virtual server; you manage the OS, patching, and scaling.\n2. Lambda is event-driven, automatically scales, and has a short execution timeout (15 min).\n3. Lambda is ideal for event-driven, short-lived tasks; EC2 for long-running, stateful apps.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. EC2 is a virtual server; you manage the OS, patching, and scaling.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. EC2 is a virtual server; you manage the OS, patching, and scaling.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
