    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLambda has service quotas such as a maximum function timeout of 15 minutes, configurable memory up to 10,240 MB, and configurable ephemeral `/tmp` storage up to 10,240 MB. Other quotas include deployment-package and concurrency limits, with exact limits depending on the deployment model and account configuration. These constraints make Lambda best suited to bounded, event-driven workloads rather than arbitrary long-running processes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Lambda has service quotas such as a maximum function timeout of 15 minutes, configurable memory up to 10,240 MB, and configurable ephemeral `/tmp` storage up to 10,240 MB.\n2. Other quotas include deployment-package and concurrency limits, with exact limits depending on the deployment model and account configuration.\n3. These constraints make Lambda best suited to bounded, event-driven workloads rather than arbitrary long-running processes.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. Lambda has service quotas such as a maximum function timeout of 15 minutes, configurable memory up to 10,240 MB, and configurable ephemeral `/tmp` storage up to 10,240 MB.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS workload, what are the limitations of aws lambda affects a specific part of the request, compute, storage, identity, network, or operations path. The design should be evaluated against availability, security, scaling, and cost requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
