    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRDS automated backups support point-in-time recovery within the configured retention period, while manual DB snapshots are user-created backups that can be retained until explicitly deleted. Automated backup retention can be configured within the supported range, while manual snapshots are useful for longer-term retention, migrations, and creating new DB instances from a known state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between automated backups and manual snapshots in rds and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. RDS automated backups support point-in-time recovery within the configured retention period, while manual DB snapshots are user-created backups that can be retained until explicitly deleted.\n2. Automated backup retention can be configured within the supported range, while manual snapshots are useful for longer-term retention, migrations, and creating new DB instances from a known state.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. RDS automated backups support point-in-time recovery within the configured retention period, while manual DB snapshots are user-created backups that can be retained until explicitly deleted.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. RDS automated backups support point-in-time recovery within the configured retention period, while manual DB snapshots are user-created backups that can be retained until explicitly deleted.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
