    question: "What is the difference between Fargate and EC2 launch types in ECS?",
    slug: "fargate-vs-ec2-launch",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nEC2 launch type gives you control over the EC2 instances (patching, scaling). Fargate is serverless; AWS manages the instances. Fargate reduces operational overhead but may be more expensive for steady workloads.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between fargate and ec2 launch types in ecs and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. EC2 launch type gives you control over the EC2 instances (patching, scaling).\n2. Fargate is serverless; AWS manages the instances.\n3. Fargate reduces operational overhead but may be more expensive for steady workloads.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nAn ECS service can run a desired number of container tasks on Fargate or EC2 capacity and integrate with a load balancer for incoming traffic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. EC2 launch type gives you control over the EC2 instances (patching, scaling).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
