    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nOn-demand has no upfront commitment and is more expensive per hour. Reserved Instances require a commitment (1 or 3 years) and offer significant discounts (up to 72%) compared to on-demand.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between on-demand and reserved instances in terms of pricing and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. On-demand has no upfront commitment and is more expensive per hour.\n2. Reserved Instances require a commitment (1 or 3 years) and offer significant discounts (up to 72%) compared to on-demand.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. On-demand has no upfront commitment and is more expensive per hour.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. On-demand has no upfront commitment and is more expensive per hour.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
