    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSNS is a pub/sub system that pushes messages to subscribers. SQS is a message queue that stores messages for consumers to pull. SNS is for fan-out; SQS is for decoupling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between sns and sqs and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SNS is a pub/sub system that pushes messages to subscribers.\n2. SQS is a message queue that stores messages for consumers to pull.\n3. SNS is for fan-out; SQS is for decoupling.\n4. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in an AWS-hosted service, a request or workload can pass through the AWS component described by the answer, and the team can verify the resulting routing, storage, or compute behavior. SNS is a pub/sub system that pushes messages to subscribers.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. SNS is a pub/sub system that pushes messages to subscribers.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
