    slug: "alb-definition",
    shortDescription: "Define L7 load balancing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn Application Load Balancer operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) and routes traffic based on path, host, or query parameters. It supports advanced routing, sticky sessions, and integration with EC2, ECS, and Lambda.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an Application Load Balancer (ALB) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An Application Load Balancer operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) and routes traffic based on path, host, or query parameters.\n2. It supports advanced routing, sticky sessions, and integration with EC2, ECS, and Lambda.\n3. Separate the service’s responsibility from the responsibilities of adjacent services, then state the main availability, IAM, networking, or cost consideration when relevant..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn ALB receives HTTP/HTTPS traffic, evaluates listener rules, and forwards requests to healthy targets in a target group.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an AWS-hosted service, this affects how traffic, compute, storage, identity, or monitoring behaves. An Application Load Balancer operates at Layer 7 (HTTP/HTTPS) and routes traffic based on path, host, or query parameters.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing services that operate at different layers.\n- Omitting the request or data flow between services.\n- Ignoring IAM, networking, availability, or cost when those constraints affect the answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nSeparate the service’s responsibility from the surrounding architecture. Mention the key boundary or limitation instead of describing AWS as a collection of interchangeable services.",
