    shortDescription: "Compare the two popular RDBMS.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMySQL is lighter and faster for read-heavy OLTP workloads, with simpler configuration. PostgreSQL is more feature-rich (advanced data types, MVCC, custom extensions) and better for complex analytical queries and data integrity. PostgreSQL is fully ACID with stricter compliance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between mysql and postgresql and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. MySQL is lighter and faster for read-heavy OLTP workloads, with simpler configuration.\n2. PostgreSQL is more feature-rich (advanced data types, MVCC, custom extensions) and better for complex analytical queries and data integrity.\n3. PostgreSQL is fully ACID with stricter compliance.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. MySQL is lighter and faster for read-heavy OLTP workloads, with simpler configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. MySQL is lighter and faster for read-heavy OLTP workloads, with simpler configuration.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
