    slug: "join-types",
    shortDescription: "List and describe.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`INNER JOIN` returns rows with matching keys in both tables. `LEFT JOIN` (or `RIGHT JOIN`) returns all rows from one side and matching rows from the other; non-matching rows are `NULL`. `CROSS JOIN` returns the Cartesian product.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because list and describe. The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `INNER JOIN` returns rows with matching keys in both tables.\n2. `LEFT JOIN` (or `RIGHT JOIN`) returns all rows from one side and matching rows from the other; non-matching rows are `NULL`.\n3. `CROSS JOIN` returns the Cartesian product.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nJoin `orders` to `customers` on `customer_id` when you need both order and customer data while keeping customer attributes normalized.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `INNER JOIN` returns rows with matching keys in both tables.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
